Asymmetric Heterogeneous Orchestration: A Technical Overview of J.A.I.S.S.
Modern distributed systems face a fundamental friction: the tension between universal portability and bare-metal performance. Traditional frameworks either abstract the hardware away—sacrificing peak compute velocity for cross-platform convenience—or lock execution into tightly coupled vendor ecosystems.

J.A.I.S.S. (Just-in-Time Autonomous Substrate Scheduler) introduces a decentralized approach to this problem: universal deployment with native execution intimacy. It is an intelligent, load-distributing orchestration engine designed to treat hardware not as a generic container, but as a precision instrument.

Core Architectural Principles
1. Hardware-Intimate Agnosticism
Rather than ignoring the underlying silicon, the engine utilizes a dynamic topology-probing layer upon initialization. While it is built to deploy seamlessly across heterogeneous environments—ranging from integrated graphics and consumer discrete GPUs to multi-node enterprise clusters—it avoids the lowest-common-denominator trap. By identifying the exact execution substrate, it unlocks specific, optimized pathways on the fly.

2. Biomimetic 80/20 Homeostasis
To eliminate runtime bloat and garbage-collection overhead, the engine relies on unmanaged memory structures and direct kernel-level memory management. Performance is characterized by absolute compute saturation paired with an internal, recursive overwatch loop:

80% Root Compute Allocation: Dedicated to maximum throughput, driving heavy payload execution at the physical limit of the silicon.

20% Recursive Overwatch: A continuous, low-level telemetry and safety validation layer that monitors pointer states, throughput velocity, and allocation boundaries in real time.

Result: The system acts as an autonomic reflex loop, self-policing its boundaries without introducing external management tax.

3. Dynamic Tiered Load-Scaling
Static workloads lead to thermal waste and resource contention. J.A.I.S.S. implements real-time payload pressure sensing that dynamically scales execution tiers:

Tier 1 (Lightweight / Edge Payloads): Bypasses heavy accelerator pipelines entirely, routing through high-velocity memory fast-paths to preserve thermal budgets and zero-out unnecessary overhead.

Tier 2 & 3 (Heavy Enterprise Payloads): Automatically escalates vector routing and unlocks maximum hardware saturation thresholds as memory and compute pressure mount, ensuring linear throughput scaling under heavy duress.

The Strategic Edge
By combining unmanaged execution speed with an autonomous, self-governing control plane, this architecture bridges the gap between raw bare-metal performance and enterprise-grade safety. It proves that software can maintain strict structural compliance and cross-platform portability without ever leaving performance on the table.
